{
  "id": "same-tree",
  "questionId": "same-tree",
  "slug": "same-tree",
  "title": "Same Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "two trees are the same if their roots match and both pairs of subtrees are also the same - what kind of recursion does that suggest?",
    "write a helper that takes an index into each array and returns whether the subtrees rooted there match",
    "first handle the 'missing node' cases: if both indices point to nothing, that's a match; if only one does, that's a mismatch",
    "if both point to real nodes, check the values are equal, then recurse on (2*i+1 vs 2*j+1) and (2*i+2 vs 2*j+2)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "only comparing the arrays element by element by index, without accounting for one array being longer or shorter than the other in a way that still represents the same shape",
    "forgetting to check both children recursively - only comparing left or only comparing right",
    "treating an out-of-bounds index the same as a valid index holding -101 inconsistently between the two trees"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees here are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array's bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node there\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 never collides with a genuine value\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2's left child is missing (sentinel) but its right child is 4.\n\nYou're given two trees, `p` and `q`, each encoded this way.\n\n**Determine whether the two trees are structurally identical and have the same value at every corresponding node.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes in each tree <= 500",
      "-100 <= Node.val <= 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "both trees empty ([-101] and [-101]), which counts as the same",
      "one tree empty and the other not, which is never the same",
      "same shape and values but a mismatch buried deep in one subtree"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "p = [1, 2, 3], q = [1, 2, 3]",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "both trees have the same root, left child, and right child values in the same positions"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "p = [1, 2], q = [1, -101, 2]",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "in p, 2 is the left child of 1; in q, 2 is the right child of 1. same values, different structure"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "p = [1, 2, 1], q = [1, 1, 2]",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "the structures match but the left and right child values are swapped"
      }
    ],
